+# Consolidated review: Essay 7 — `chapters/07-information-farming.html`
 
-Verdict: **BLOCK**.
+**BLOCK — revise before shipping: the ledger misreads linguistic engineering, overstates the p. 243 HIT, uses FunSearch as though it were coupled to a real phenomenon, carries obsolete A-Lab facts, and omits Lem's own note 8, which changes the chapter's answer to the information-deluge problem.**
 
-Key conclusions:
+Findings are ranked by severity. Reviewer labels refer to `07-grok.md` (G1–G12) and `07-agy.md` (A1–A6).
 
-- Both MISS rows fail CONTEXT §4.
-- Lem rejected maximum random diversity on p.261, not filtering itself. Modern FunSearch uses structured prompts, program skeletons, and evolutionary selection—not Ashby’s pure noise generator. [Nature](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06924-6)
-- The central claim that neither Lem nor Ashby thought selection was hard contradicts p.261.
-- The “no automated experiment anywhere” baseline is false; a Robot Chemist existed in 1959. [ACS](https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/cen-v037n041.p078)
-- All eight ledger rows and all nine Agy findings are explicitly adjudicated; Grok produced no substantive findings.
-- The report also identifies layer violations, semantic claim-register mismatches, unmarked present-day claims, three unregistered Lem fragments, and the p.260–261 attribution correction.
- Agy 2 — p.264 sieve HIT omits defining context | **CONFIRMED** | The molecular encoding and isodynamic carriers are integral to the described sieve; A-Lab is not substantially that mechanism. |
-| Agy 3 — p.241 “plagiarizer” is a missed MISS | **REJECTED** | Lem means an exact, all-variable copy; generative models are selective, lossy abstractions. |
-| Agy 4 — selection row lacks problem-level integrity | **CONFIRMED** | The row conflates Ashby’s selection-only position with Lem’s p.259 fitness rule. Agy’s proposed MISS and claim that generation is the hardest part remain unsupported. |
-| Agy 5 — third-language row is in the wrong layer | **CONFIRMED** | It forecasts an object/system and belongs under Object level. |
-| Agy 6 — combinatorial wall is a reductio/MISS | **REJECTED** | It is not a reductio, and Lem explicitly supplies linkage as the escape clause. The current HIT is separately unverified by its receipt. |
-| Agy 7 — 1964 perceptron baseline is wrong | **CONFIRMED** | “Read” must be narrowed to reading text; the register does not support it, and the baseline has a larger false automation claim. |
-| Agy 8 — `gpt3-2020` does not receipt tool use | **CONFIRMED** | GPT-3 pays only the text-trained competence clause; tool action, search loops, and opacity require other adjacent receipts. |
-| Agy 9 — ledger/rhetoric mismatch | **CONFIRMED** | The skeptic’s objection is valid, although Agy is wrong to assume the molecular design is already a defensible “complete MISS.” |
+## 1. Lem's note 8 supplies the chapter's missing second sieve and reverses the last OPEN row — MISSED BY BOTH
 
-## Ranked findings
+**Location:** the whole essay, especially “the sieve has to be wired to the phenomenon,” the problem-level row “Automating production as the answer to the information deluge,” and the retraction/paper-mill discussion.
 
-### 1. Both MISS rows are invalid as written — CONFIRMED (missed by both reviewers)
+**Finding:** On pp. 365–367, Lem explicitly says an information farm may “exacerbate, rather than diminish” the information crisis; cannot decide what a civilization ought to do; may become a “gigabyte” bomb; and, without an effective “addressing sieve,” will produce a paper deluge that makes further research impossible. He even forecasts insignificant papers produced for degrees or ambition and says automated librarianship and publishing selection are more urgent than open-ended cognition. The essay uses the 2023 retraction record as evidence that Lem's remedy remains untested while omitting a passage in which Lem predicts substantially that failure mode.
 
-**Location:** ledger rows “The carriers … MISS” and “His bet against Ashby … MISS”; lede, “the machine does not exist and will not” and “The filter won.”
+This is not a side note. It distinguishes two selectors:
 
-The polymer-carrier row does not meet §4’s definition of MISS: “did not happen **and there is no live path**.” Its only receipt is `funsearch-2023`, which proves that a digital competitor exists; it says nothing about the absence of a live molecular path. Lem also introduces synthetic polymers only “for example” (p.263), calls the farm “extremely difficult” rather than impossible, and on p.266 varies the possible substance and pseudo-organism form before conceding that the ideas cannot then be verified. Active molecular computation makes “will not” especially unsafe; even without adding new evidence, the registered receipt cannot pay that absence claim. Make the exact isodynamic molecular farm **OPEN**, unless a source can establish that its required mechanism has no live path. “Not built” alone is not MISS.
+- a **truth sieve**, coupled to the phenomenon (pp. 259, 264); and
+- an **addressing sieve**, coupled to the civilization's questions and capacity to use the answer (pp. 365–367).
 
-The Ashby row misstates both sides of the comparison. On p.261 Lem explicitly says that his position differs, but the distinction is **greatest, random/noise diversity** versus **significant, limited diversity that develops**. He does not reject filtering as such: his own farm repeatedly generates variants and passes them through “sieves.” FunSearch is not Ashby’s air-molecule generator either. It begins from a program skeleton, samples a structured LLM, preserves a diverse pool, and iteratively evolves high-scoring survivors; GNoME uses trained graph networks and active-learning filtration. Those receipts prove generate/evaluate loops, not that maximum random diversity beat structured development. Remove this MISS or rewrite the row around the precise p.261 distinction and grade it **OPEN** on current evidence.
+The essay's declared “one rule” is therefore only half of Lem's rule. It also makes “he never asks what happens when … a proxy gets substituted” too broad: he does not discuss slow or costly experiments, but he does discuss selection becoming detached from human relevance and drowning in its own output.
 
-These failures matter beyond two cells: the essay was pitched around two honest misses, but neither presently survives §4.
+**Fix:** Bring note 8 into “What Lem said,” split the sieve analysis into truth and addressing, and add a problem-level row for the forecast that automated knowledge production without addressing will worsen the deluge. Rebuild the final OPEN row after that; pp. 281 and 237–239 do not support its current wording.
 
-### 2. The essay’s central claim about selection reverses the corpus — CONFIRMED (missed by both reviewers)
+## 2. The A-Lab evidence is obsolete and now cuts against the essay's proxy-failure story — G9 CONFIRMED
 
-**Location:** lede, “the part neither of them thought was hard”; “the part Lem treated as machinery — the sieve — is the part that broke”; ledger row “Selection, not production, is where the value enters.”
+**Location:** “41 novel compounds from 58 targets”; “outside crystallographers … concluded that no new materials had been made”; ledger row on the selector.
 
-Ashby’s position on p.261 is that “the only things that really matter are elimination and selection.” Lem likewise spends pp.260–265 specifying an externally coupled selector and says on p.263 that setting up the farm is “extremely difficult.” The essay may fairly argue that neither anticipated **proxy validation** as the modern failure mode, but it cannot say that neither thought selection mattered or was hard. Rebuild the thesis narrowly: they foregrounded selection, but Lem’s proposed truth test assumed a reliable coupling to the phenomenon and did not analyze proxy failure.
+**Finding:** The current Nature record no longer supports those numbers or that formulation. The January 2026 author correction says novelty meant new to the prediction platform, not necessarily new to science, and reports post-publication confirmation of 36 of 40 claimed successes; the corrected paper now reports 36 compounds from 57 targets. The essay and `checks/claims-2026.tsv` still carry 41/58 and treat the 2024 critique as though it established failure to synthesize rather than a dispute over novelty and phase identification. [Nature's correction](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09992-y.pdf) and [corrected article](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06734-w) supersede the receipt.
 
-The ledger row compounds the problem. “Selection, not production” belongs to Ashby’s p.261 position, while its quoted support—“that which expresses the environment most adequately survives” (p.259)—is Lem’s fitness rule, not a claim that production is trivial. Re-anchor and reword the row before deciding its verdict.
+This matters to the argument, not just the arithmetic: a 36/40 post-publication confirmation does not cleanly demonstrate that a proxy selector “wobbled.”
 
-### 3. The 1964 baseline is partly false and mostly unreceipted — CONFIRMED; expands Agy 7
+**Fix:** Update the prose and claim rows to the corrected 36/57 and 36/40 figures; distinguish “new to the platform” from “new to science”; state exactly which four results remained inconclusive and what the external critique still disputes. Reassess whether A-Lab remains a negative example in the sieve row.
 
-**Location:** “perceptrons that could not yet read, machines doing a million operations a second, and no automated experiment anywhere. `baseline-1964`”
+## 3. The p. 243 functional HIT is inflated by an ellipsis — G1 CONFIRMED; A2 REJECTED
 
-The register row, filed under essay 4 rather than essay 7, receipts only the existence/dates of Beer, Ashby, Wiener, and Rosenblatt’s 1958 perceptron. It does not support the reading limitation, the operations-per-second number, or the global absence claim. The Mark I could classify simple visual patterns, including letters; “could not yet read” needs narrowing to Lem’s own p.153 claim that then-current perceptrons could not “read texts.” More seriously, “no automated experiment anywhere” is false: automatic titrators and clinical analyzers predated 1964, including the 1956 AutoAnalyzer and the 1959 Robot Chemist. Replace the sentence with a receipted distinction between automated measurement/analysis and autonomous hypothesis-generation, experimentation, and revision.
+**Location:** first object-level row, “A device that will gather information, generalize it…”.
 
-### 4. The two object-level HITs are inflated, but Agy’s proposed alternatives need correction — CONFIRMED (Agy 1–2)
+**Finding:** The corpus gives the load-bearing clause as “generalize it **in the same way the scientist does**” and immediately adds “Our device thus **produces theories**” (p. 243). The ledger's ellipsis removes the first clause and its evidence never confronts the second. Adam generated and tested bounded gene-function hypotheses; Coscientist planned and optimized reactions; the AI Scientist performed computational ML research. These establish increasingly complete scientific loops, but not plainly Lem's device producing theories in the full sense he defines on pp. 243–244. “At a smaller scope” in the prose does not narrow the ledger claim.
 
-**Location:** ledger rows “Automating hypothesis formation … p.239 — HIT” and “A ‘sieve’ … p.264 — HIT.”
+G1 is therefore confirmed: the full sentence is not paid by a clean HIT. A2's proposed **RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON** is rejected, however. The ledger deliberately scores the functional specification separately from the substrate bet. Importing the mechanism into this row would make it compound and double-score p. 251. The right correction is to narrow the claim to a demonstrated gather–hypothesize–test loop or grade the full claim **EARLY**.
 
-On p.239 Lem says that “many cyberneticists are currently working” on automatic hypothesis formation, and note 1 cites Samuel Amarel’s 1962 “An Approach to Automatic Theory Formation.” This is a report of an existing program, not Lem’s own forecast. Agy is right that the row cannot pay a predictive HIT from p.239, but wrong to suggest DISSOLVED or OPEN: neither verdict describes the problem. Re-anchor the row to Lem’s own p.243 forecast of a device that gathers facts, generalizes, checks the generalization on new facts, and presents the result to experts. That claim can plausibly take HIT against Adam/Coscientist/AI Scientist, stated at the demonstrated scope.
+The AI Scientist did undergo real human workshop review; it was not merely scored by its own automated reviewer. One of three submissions cleared the workshop threshold and was withdrawn under a pre-agreed protocol. The essay should say that Nature published the human-authored system paper, not the generated workshop paper. [Lu et al.](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10265-5)
 
-The p.264 “sieve” row abstracts away too much to count as “substantially as described.” In context, the sieve encodes the phenomenon’s state into molecular specks and physically selects isodynamic, developing carriers. A-Lab automated synthesis and interpretation, but it did not implement that mechanism or an evolutionary survivor loop; its interpretation was also the disputed part. Either merge this into the molecular-farm row or anchor a narrower HIT to p.243’s gather–generalize–test–quality-control loop. Agy’s proposed RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER is possible only if the row is moved to the problem layer and names the live validation dilemma.
+**Fix:** Restore the omitted words and “produces theories.” Either narrow the 2026 side and keep HIT only for the bounded loop, or retain Lem's full specification and use EARLY. Clarify the withdrawal and the identity of the Nature paper.
 
-### 5. The information-deluge verdict is not proved by its receipts — CONFIRMED (missed by both reviewers)
+## 4. The third-language row splices two different forecasts and misdefines “causal” — G2 and G11 CONFIRMED
 
-**Location:** “Automating production as the answer to the information deluge … RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER.”
+**Location:** “a competence grown outside a brain, from written language, now acts on the world through tools”; fourth object row, pp. 277 and 280.
 
-`retractions-2023` shows a record retraction year driven largely by compromised peer review and paper mills; it does not show that automated scientific production caused the deluge or failed as its remedy. `ai-scientist-2026` shows one of three generated submissions clearing a 70%-acceptance workshop after human selection of submissions; the paper discusses risks of noise and review overload, but a reported risk is not an event. Under AGENT.md’s reported-versus-true rule, this row is **OPEN** unless the essay adds evidence that automated production has worsened the information crisis or failed to tame it.
+**Finding:** In Lem's scale, ordinary speech already has both causal and comprehending aspects (pp. 267–268). Purely causal language is hereditary language in which the utterance itself materializes its content—“word literally becomes flesh” (pp. 273–274). An LLM emitting text that a separate tool runner executes is not thereby the “causal language of the next kind” forecast on p. 280.
 
-### 6. The combinatorial-wall HIT is unverified, although Agy’s “reductio” reading is wrong — UNVERIFIABLE current verdict; Agy 6 REJECTED
+The essay also joins different claims. Page 277 forecasts a bifurcation in which causal language **stops being comprehending** and science produces predictions without explanations. Page 280 forecasts a richer causal language that, after natural language develops autonomous extracerebral tools, “almost by accident” crosses a threshold of understanding. The current sentence makes p. 277 sound like the output of p. 280 and then pays both with GPT-3 plus tool use. It also turns essay 4's recorded omission—training on text at scale as the route to competence—into a credit for Lem here.
 
-**Location:** “Combinatorial wall … HIT”; `scaling-laws-2020`.
+G11 is confirmed as a separate ledger omission: pp. 276–277 contain the chapter's direct, testable forecast that control will outrun understanding and predictions will become detached from explanations, but the ledger never scores it on its own.
 
-Pp.239–240 do not present a reductio that Lem later knocks down. Lem qualifies exhaustive enumeration to variables “as long as we cannot see any link between them,” then argues that hierarchy and selection of significant variables avoid ultimate modelling. Agy therefore cannot turn success through linkage into a MISS; Lem wrote that escape clause himself.
+**Fix:** Split p. 277 from p. 280. Score control/prediction without explanation against appropriate scientific systems. Leave the p. 280 next-kind causal language OPEN or EARLY only with evidence that matches Lem's materializing sense of causality. Keep text-at-scale as the omission recorded in essay 4.
 
-The ledger still fails independently. `scaling-laws-2020` receipts smooth power-law improvement of language-model loss with parameters, data, and compute. It does not establish that millions of handled variables are linked, that nobody handles more than several dozen independent variables, or Lem’s universal ceiling. Add an appropriate complexity/statistics receipt and define “deal with,” or grade the argument OPEN. The current HIT is not verified by its register row.
+## 5. FunSearch is not a sieve “connected … to a real phenomenon” — G3 CONFIRMED
 
-### 7. The language row violates the layer split and its receipts are semantically mismatched — CONFIRMED (Agy 5 and 8)
+**Location:** “Where the evaluator runs the world, results hold (FunSearch)” and the problem-level sieve HIT.
 
-**Location:** problem-level row “A third language … EARLY”; prose, “grown outside a brain, from written language, now acts on the world through tools … outputs we can use and cannot follow. `gpt3-2020`”
+**Finding:** Lem's sieve receives structural information from an external phenomenon and molecularly compares a carrier's predicted state with that phenomenon's actual state (pp. 259–260, 263–264). FunSearch executes a program against a formal evaluator for cap sets or bin packing. Its score is determinate, but it is not “the world” in Lem's stated sense. The essay then correctly uses FunSearch as the easy, cheap-to-evaluate contrast to physical materials work, contradicting the ledger's use of it as the positive empirical case.
 
-This is an object-level forecast of a system/language, not a problem-level dilemma. Move it to Object level. EARLY is plausible, but the evidence must be split. `gpt3-2020` supports web-scale text training and few-shot behavior; it does not support tool use, search loops, causal action, or opacity. `ai-scientist-2026` supports an agentic tool-using pipeline, and `funsearch-2023` supports the search-loop generator, but the prose does not place those markers on this sentence and the ledger omits FunSearch’s marker. “Cannot follow” needs an interpretability receipt. Agy’s chronology language is imprecise—the essay does not claim that GPT-3 itself used tools—but its receipt-mismatch finding is correct.
+The row is legitimately problem-level if it is explicitly framed as a general design rule; G3 overstates the layer objection on that point. Its main objection is nevertheless confirmed because the evidence does not instantiate the quoted rule.
 
-### 8. The 2026 register has additional semantic failures and unmarked claims — CONFIRMED (missed by both reviewers)
+**Fix:** Use a genuinely empirical closed loop for the world-coupled case and treat FunSearch separately as formal search with an exact evaluator. Reassess A-Lab after the 2026 correction rather than using it as the automatic counterexample.
 
-The remaining essay-7 rows broadly match their carefully qualified prose: `adam-2009`, `coscientist-2023`, `alab-2023`, `ai-scientist-2026`, `funsearch-2023`, `gnome-2023`, `alab-critique-2024`, and `retractions-2023`. Adam is “described as” first; A-Lab is “reported”; the critique is presented as a conclusion, not settled fact. The 2026 correction to the A-Lab paper should nevertheless be added to the register so the current state of the dispute is visible.
+## 6. The p. 251 row is compound and misplaced, but the reviewers' proposed MISS is not in Lem — G5 and A4 CONFIRMED; G4 and A3 REJECTED
 
-The following do not match:
+**Location:** second object-level row; last problem-level OPEN row; “a digital version … was buildable in 1964.”
 
-- `gnome-duplicates-2025` supports duplicate findings and calls for correction/retraction, but not “a citation count in the thousands.” The cited report said about 1,000 citations. Cut the number or receipt it accurately.
-- Calling GNoME “industrial scale” is not in `gnome-2023`; it is a large research computation, not evidence of industrial deployment.
-- `baseline-1964`, `scaling-laws-2020`, and `gpt3-2020` are registered as essay-4 claims and reused here for materially broader essay-7 claims. The checker ignores the essay column and semantic drift.
+**Finding:** G5/A4 are confirmed. The row combines (a) formal representation causing an information crisis and (b) embryogenesis as the proposed escape, but its receipt only shows that some automation arrived within digital formalism. Under the book's own definition, **RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER** grades a live dilemma and belongs at problem level. Page 281 is not “automating production”; it introduces transcendence engineering as “another possibility” for taming the deluge, then argues against it.
 
-Material present-day claims with no adequate adjacent marker include: the farm “will not” exist/no live path; “nobody” handles more than a few dozen independent variables; systems handle millions only because they are linked; model outputs are usable but unfollowable; 2026 automation is “uniformly” optimized for production at rates no community can absorb; the gardener bargain is normal in “three or four fields”; search and testing are cheap; the digital loop was already possible in 1964; “nothing in 2026 climbs”; and cosmogonic engineering has no live path “in any form.” Receipt, narrow, or cut each.
+G4/A3 are rejected. Lem does not say digital computers will fail to automate any science or reach a dead end. In context (pp. 248–251), “information crisis” includes the human brain's input bottleneck and the impossibility of exhaustively writing a molecular “score for embryogenesis.” That crisis is not disproved by Adam or FunSearch. Page 263 says signs **on a piece of paper** cannot interact; executable computer state is not a literal counterexample to that sentence, and present systems still do not produce Lem's general theory hierarchy from empirical phenomena. Calling this a flat MISS would manufacture a stronger forecast than Lem made.
 
-### 9. Three inline Lem quotations are not registered; direct-quote page numbers otherwise pass — CONFIRMED (missed by both reviewers)
+The unmarked claim that the same digital loop “was buildable in 1964” is also an unsupported counterfactual; the cited historical baseline does not establish it.
 
-**Location:** “taking up ‘food’” (p.263), “a ‘sieve’” (p.264, prose and ledger), and Ashby’s “‘generator of diversity’” (pp.260–261).
+**Fix:** Move and split the p. 251 row. Grade the route choice without declaring that the information crisis disappeared. Cut or receipt the 1964 counterfactual. Do not add the reviewers' proposed MISS.
 
-These quoted Lem terms do not have their own `checks/quotes.tsv` rows and are not contained in a longer registered excerpt that gates them. Add rows. Section titles in quotation marks can be treated as titles rather than quotations, but the checker should eventually gain an HTML-to-ledger reverse pass.
+## 7. The combinatorial-wall HIT is paid by evidence from the wrong domain — G6 CONFIRMED
 
-All registered essay-7 excerpts were found on their stated page (allowing the intentional p.251–252 split), and I found no outright wrong printed-page number. One attribution should be made more exact: the air-molecule setup begins on p.260, while Ashby’s conclusion and Lem’s response are on p.261, so cite pp.260–261. The problem at p.261 is interpretation, not pagination.
+**Location:** first problem-level row; “Nobody enumerates a few dozen genuinely independent variables.”
 
-### 10. “Universal plagiarizer” is not a missing MISS — REJECTED (Agy 3)
+**Finding:** Lem's claim concerns hypothesis formation over variables whose relations are not known (p. 239). Pope et al. measures the intrinsic dimension of image datasets. Showing that natural images occupy a low-dimensional manifold does not show that no system can handle more than several dozen independent scientific variables, nor does it test Lem's numerical ceiling. The current HIT reduces to the truism that exhaustive search grows combinatorially and then illustrates it with a different task.
 
-P.241 does not claim that learning from or recombining a large corpus prevents creativity. It says that an **exact material copy containing all variables** ceases to be a model and cannot perform the variable selection that theory and creativity require. Generative models are aggressively lossy statistical abstractions, not exact copies of every variable in the phenomena they model. Agy’s proposed MISS reverses Lem’s argument; if graded at all, the modern analogy tends toward HIT or remains an ungraded conceptual parallel.
+The two present-day universals—“Nobody enumerates…” and systems handle millions only because variables are dependent—are not separately receipted.
 
-### 11. Agy’s generator-versus-selector remedy is unsupported, though the row needs repair — Agy 4 CONFIRMED narrowly
+**Fix:** Narrow the row to the combinatorics of exhaustive enumeration and source that exact claim, or grade Lem's scientific-variable ceiling OPEN. Do not use image manifolds as direct confirmation.
 
-Agy correctly detects that the row’s wording and evidence do not cleanly score one Lem problem-level claim. It does not establish its stronger assertion that the generator is “the hardest and most valuable part,” nor does that assertion make Lem’s p.259 fitness criterion a MISS. FunSearch itself says the target problems are hard to solve but easy to evaluate, while also treating the evaluator as essential. Fix the attribution described in finding 2; do not substitute Agy’s unsupported MISS.
+## 8. One OPEN row combines a contemporary practice with Lem's unbuilt machine hierarchy — G10 CONFIRMED
 
-### 12. The ledger is rhetorically gerrymandered, but not for the reason Agy gives — Agy 9 CONFIRMED in result, REJECTED in rationale
+**Location:** “Scientists will ‘build their theories not from facts but from other theories’ … climbing to ‘a theory of the class of classes.’”
 
-Specific component rows are required by CONTEXT §4, so atomizing a design is not inherently dishonest, and Agy cannot call the molecular design a “complete MISS” without proving no live path. The skeptical-reader problem is real for a different reason: two generic components receive HITs after their defining context is removed, while both advertised MISS rows fail the vocabulary test. After the corrections above, rebuild the ledger first and then make the prose match it.
+**Finding:** Page 243 describes what human scientists will do with machine output and immediately says theory-on-theory construction already happens to some extent. Page 265 describes successive machine-carrier generations culminating in a theory of classes. Different actors, baselines and closure conditions are collapsed into one OPEN row.
 
-## Complete ledger disposition
+**Fix:** Split the already-partly-present human practice from the machine hierarchy. Grade only the latter against machine-made generalizations over theories.
 
